**1. A method of configuring a mobile device to remotely control a hearing aid, the method comprising:**

detecting, by the mobile device, a removable communication module received in a Secure Digital (SD) card interface of the mobile device, the removable communication module comprising a wireless transceiver and storing a module identifier;

reading, by the mobile device through the SD card interface, the module identifier;

transmitting, by the mobile device to an authorization server, an authorization request comprising the module identifier;

receiving, by the mobile device from the authorization server, an authorization response generated based on verification of the module identifier against authorization data maintained by the authorization server;

in response to the authorization response indicating that the removable communication module is authorized, configuring the mobile device to enable a hearing-aid control interface and to use the wireless transceiver of the removable communication module for communication with the hearing aid;

receiving, through the hearing-aid control interface, a user input specifying an adjustment to an operating parameter of the hearing aid;

generating a control command corresponding to the adjustment; and

transmitting the control command from the removable communication module to the hearing aid, thereby causing the hearing aid to modify the operating parameter.

## Title

**Mobile User Device Configured as a Remote Controller for a Hearing Assistance Device Using a Radio-Enabled Memory Card**

## Technical Field

The present disclosure relates generally to hearing assistance systems and, more particularly, to systems and methods for enabling a mobile user device to remotely control one or more hearing aids by obtaining hearing-aid configuration information from a remote server and configuring a radio interface associated with a removable memory card, such as a Secure Digital (SD) card.

## Background

Hearing aids commonly include programmable operating parameters, including amplification gain, frequency response, microphone directionality, noise reduction, feedback cancellation, listening programs, volume, and environmental adaptation settings. Adjustment of such parameters may require a dedicated remote-control device or specialized fitting equipment.

Mobile user devices, including mobile telephones, smartphones, tablet computers, and portable media devices, provide user interfaces and network connectivity suitable for controlling hearing aids. However, a mobile user device may not natively support the radio protocol or frequency band used by a particular hearing aid. Consequently, a user may be required to carry a separate remote controller or an external radio accessory.

Accordingly, there is a need for a system that enables a mobile user device to communicate with and control a hearing aid without requiring the mobile user device to include a native hearing-aid radio interface.

## Summary of the Invention

In one aspect, a system is provided in which a mobile user device is converted into a remote controller for at least one hearing aid. The mobile user device receives hearing-aid settings from a remote server through a communications network. The settings may include radio-interface configuration data, hearing-aid identification information, pairing credentials, control definitions, fitting parameters, and one or more user-selectable hearing programs.

An SD card having a radio interface is removably coupled to the mobile user device. An application executed by the mobile user device configures the radio interface of the SD card in accordance with at least a portion of the retrieved hearing-aid settings. Once configured, the SD card establishes a wireless communication link with the hearing aid. The mobile user device may thereafter transmit commands to the hearing aid and may receive status information from the hearing aid through the SD card.

The disclosed arrangement allows a general-purpose mobile user device to operate as a hearing-aid remote controller even when the device does not natively support the wireless communication protocol used by the hearing aid.

## Detailed Description

A hearing assistance system comprises a mobile user device, a removable SD card, at least one hearing aid, and a remote server. The mobile user device may comprise a smartphone, mobile telephone, tablet computer, personal digital assistant, portable media player, or other processor-based portable apparatus having an SD-card interface or a compatible removable-card interface.

The mobile user device includes a processor, memory, a graphical user interface, a network communications interface, and a card interface. The network communications interface may communicate with the remote server through a cellular network, a wireless local-area network, the Internet, or another data network.

The SD card includes a memory controller, nonvolatile memory, and a radio subsystem. The radio subsystem may comprise a radio-frequency transceiver, an antenna, and control circuitry. In certain embodiments, the radio subsystem supports a low-power proprietary hearing-aid protocol. In other embodiments, the radio subsystem supports Bluetooth Low Energy, near-field magnetic induction, an inductive radio protocol, or another short-range wireless protocol used by the hearing aid. The radio subsystem may be programmable with respect to one or more of operating frequency, channel selection, modulation, transmit power, packet format, network address, encryption parameters, pairing information, and communication timing.

A remote-control application is installed or otherwise executed by the mobile user device. Upon initiation, the application identifies the user, the hearing aid, or both. Identification may be performed using account credentials, a hearing-aid serial number, a device identifier, a scanned optical code, a near-field communication exchange, or information previously stored in the mobile user device or the SD card.

The application transmits a request to the remote server for settings associated with the identified hearing aid. The server may maintain a database containing hearing-aid profiles generated by a hearing-care professional, a manufacturer, or the user. A profile may include:

1. a hearing-aid model or device identifier;
2. left-ear and right-ear designations;
3. wireless protocol parameters;
4. pairing or authentication credentials;
5. permissible remote-control functions;
6. gain, volume, or frequency-response limits;
7. selectable listening programs;
8. microphone mode and directionality settings;
9. noise-reduction and feedback-management settings;
10. tinnitus-masking settings;
11. environmental adaptation parameters; and
12. firmware or compatibility information.

The server authenticates the request and returns an applicable hearing-aid profile to the mobile user device. The returned data may be encrypted, digitally signed, or otherwise protected against unauthorized modification. The mobile user device may verify the authenticity and integrity of the profile before applying the profile.

The application determines whether the inserted SD card is capable of communicating with the identified hearing aid. If the SD card is compatible, the application transfers at least the required radio configuration data to the SD card through the card interface. The SD card then configures its radio subsystem according to the transferred data. Configuration may include loading radio firmware, selecting a communication protocol, setting a carrier frequency or channel, assigning a device address, storing cryptographic keys, and establishing communication timing appropriate for the hearing aid.

After configuration, the SD card initiates discovery, pairing, or connection establishment with the hearing aid. The hearing aid may authenticate the SD card using a shared secret, a certificate, a challenge-response procedure, or a user-confirmed pairing operation. For a binaural system, the SD card may establish respective links with left and right hearing aids or may communicate with one hearing aid that relays commands to the other hearing aid.

The mobile user device presents a remote-control interface corresponding to the retrieved profile. For example, the interface may include controls for changing volume, selecting a listening program, adjusting directional microphone operation, muting the hearing aid, modifying a sound-balance setting, checking battery status, or locating the hearing aid. Available controls may be limited by permissions specified in the profile so that safety-critical fitting parameters cannot be changed beyond limits established by a hearing-care professional.

When the user operates a control, the application generates a command and passes the command to the SD card. The radio subsystem formats and wirelessly transmits the command to the hearing aid using the configured protocol. The hearing aid applies the requested setting and may return an acknowledgement or updated status information. The SD card provides the acknowledgement or status information to the application, which updates the displayed user interface.

In some embodiments, the application stores a local copy of the hearing-aid profile, thereby permitting remote-control operation when the remote server is unavailable. The server may assign an expiration time or version identifier to the local copy. When network connectivity is subsequently available, the application may determine whether an updated profile exists and may synchronize changes with the server.

The mobile user device may also transmit usage information to the server, subject to user authorization. Such information may include selected programs, volume adjustments, detected acoustic environments, hearing-aid battery status, connection diagnostics, and error reports. A hearing-care professional may review the information and generate an updated profile. The updated profile may then be downloaded by the mobile user device and applied to the SD card and hearing aid.

In certain implementations, the SD card retains multiple radio profiles corresponding to different hearing aids. The application selects a profile based on the hearing aid to be controlled. The SD card may reconfigure its radio subsystem dynamically when the user selects a different hearing aid or when a compatible hearing aid is detected nearby.

Although an SD card is described, the invention may be implemented using a microSD card, miniSD card, embedded secure element, removable memory module, or another accessory that is coupled to a host-device interface and includes both storage and configurable radio functionality.

## Exemplary Method

A method for operating a mobile user device as a hearing-aid remote controller comprises:

1. detecting a radio-enabled SD card coupled to the mobile user device;
2. identifying at least one hearing aid associated with a user;
3. transmitting, by the mobile user device, a request for hearing-aid settings to a remote server;
4. receiving a hearing-aid profile from the remote server;
5. verifying the hearing-aid profile;
6. extracting radio configuration data from the hearing-aid profile;
7. configuring a radio interface of the SD card using the radio configuration data;
8. establishing, through the configured radio interface, a wireless link with the hearing aid;
9. receiving a user control input at the mobile user device;
10. transmitting a corresponding control command from the SD card to the hearing aid; and
11. presenting hearing-aid status information at the mobile user device.

## Advantages

The disclosed invention may eliminate the need for a dedicated hearing-aid remote controller, extend hearing-aid compatibility to mobile devices lacking a native hearing-aid radio, allow server-managed personalization, support professional control of safe adjustment limits, and provide a single user interface for configuring and monitoring one or more hearing aids.

1. **A method for controlling access to hearing-aid configuration data, the method comprising:**

   receiving, by a mobile phone, a removable secure digital card installed in a card interface of the mobile phone, the removable secure digital card comprising a radio interface, an antenna, and an antenna controller configured to control operation of the antenna;

   obtaining, by the mobile phone, an identifier associated with the removable secure digital card;

   determining, based at least in part on the identifier, whether the removable secure digital card is authorized to access hearing-aid configuration data;

   in response to determining that the removable secure digital card is authorized, enabling access to the hearing-aid configuration data and controlling, by the antenna controller, the antenna to communicate the hearing-aid configuration data via the radio interface between the mobile phone and a hearing aid; and

   in response to determining that the removable secure digital card is not authorized, preventing access to the hearing-aid configuration data.

In one embodiment, a software-enabled mobile device comprises a processor, a memory storing executable instructions, and a communication module configured for wireless communication with a hearing aid. Execution of the instructions causes the processor to transmit, to the communication module, a request for an identifier associated with the communication module. In response to the request, the processor receives the identifier from the communication module and transmits the identifier, via a network interface, to a remote server.

The remote server authenticates or otherwise validates the identifier and, based at least in part on the validation, identifies hearing-aid setting information authorized for use with the communication module, the mobile device, a user account, or a hearing aid associated therewith. The mobile device receives the authorized hearing-aid setting information from the remote server and applies at least a portion of the authorized hearing-aid setting information to configure one or more wireless-communication parameters. The configured parameters may include a communication protocol, frequency or channel selection, pairing data, security credentials, transmission power, audio-streaming parameters, codec parameters, latency parameters, or hearing-aid profile information. The mobile device thereafter establishes or controls wireless communication with the hearing aid in accordance with the applied setting information.

**Example claim language:**

A computer-implemented method comprising:

1. transmitting, by a processor of a mobile device, a request to a communication module of the mobile device for an identifier associated with the communication module;
2. receiving, by the processor, the identifier from the communication module;
3. transmitting the identifier from the mobile device to a remote server;
4. receiving, from the remote server and responsive to authorization based at least in part on the identifier, hearing-aid setting information; and
5. configuring wireless communication between the mobile device and a hearing aid using at least a portion of the hearing-aid setting information.
